```js
/// <reference path="../pb_data/types.d.ts" />
migrate((app) => {
  let collection = new Collection({
    name: "tasks",
    type: "base",
    fields: [
      { name: "title", type: "text", required: true },
      { name: "done", type: "bool" },
      { name: "due", type: "date" },
    ],
  });
  app.save(collection);
}, (app) => {
  let collection = app.findCollectionByNameOrId("tasks");
  app.delete(collection);
});
```

Wait, I should make the migration idempotent too. Actually, `migrate up` only runs migrations that haven't been applied yet (tracked in `_migrations` table). So the migration itself only runs once. That handles idempotency for the collection creation. But if I want extra safety, I could check if the collection exists before creating. But since migrations are tracked, it's fine. However, if the DB is fresh and migration ran, then on second script run, migrate up won't re-run it. Good.

But there's a subtle issue: what if the collection already exists from a previous run but the migration history doesn't have it (e.g., manual deletion)? That's an edge case I won't worry about. The migration tracking handles the normal idempotency.

